 from the ATR/QM rule for conforming prime residential mortgages (answers 3, 4, or 5 to questions 17a or 17b), please indicate how important the designation of a temporary class of qualified mortgages is in your answer.<a href="#fn3"><sup>3</sup></a><a name="fn3r"></a>  This temporary classification automatically considers any mortgage loan that is accepted by the government-sponsored enterprises&#8217; automated underwriting engines as a qualified mortgage even if some of the QM provisions (such as the debt-to-income ratio not exceeding 43 percent) are not met.                </p></p><center><table border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="6" summary="19. If you indicated no effect or a higher approval rate from the ATR/QM rule for conforming prime residential mortgages (answers 3, 4, or 5 to questions Home purchase loans that your bank categorizes as prime residential mortgages (as described in questions and ) with principal balances less than or equal to the conforming loan limits announced by the FHFAPlease include mortgages in high-cost areas with loan balances greater than $417,000 that are within the area-specific conforming loan limits. (up to $625,500 for fiscal year 2014) determined under the Housing and Economic Recovery Act of 2008. For more information on conforming loan limits, please see: http://www.fhfa.gov/DataTools/Downloads/Pages/Conforming-Loan-Limits.aspx. and associated with a borrower who has a FICO score (or equivalent) of less than or equal to 680 and Home purchase loans that your bank categorizes as prime residential mortgages (as described in questions and ) with principal balances less than or equal to the conforming loan limits announced by the FHFA and associated with a borrower who has a FICO score (or equivalent) of greater than 680), please indicate how important the designation of a temporary class of qualified mortgages is in your answer. This temporary classification automatically considers any mortgage loan that is accepted by the 